 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Octree-based out-of-core volume rendering](5171-octree-based-out-of-core-volume-rendering.md)
-An extension supporting multiple visible channels by storing pointers and culling metadata in each node. It enforces whole-subtree residency for high-resolution rendering, leading to a higher memory footprint than the [Residency Octree](4950-residency-octree.md) but comparable performance for single-channel data.
+Extends the single-channel approach to support multiple visible channels by storing pointers and culling metadata in each node. Enforces whole subtree residency for high-resolution rendering, leading to higher memory footprint and worse performance for multiple channels compared to the [Residency Octree](4950-residency-octree.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Depends on the tree traversal and the number of loaded bricks. Breadth-first storage enables fast recursive refinements [S2, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Physical memory usage is proportional to the number of bricks actually rendered, not the total dataset size [S2, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Efficient use of physical memory by only loading rendered parts; progressive loading; fast recursive refinements due to breadth-first storage [S2, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Limited to 4 channels due to GPU texture formats; potential occlusion artifacts in multi-channel rendering [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** Suitable for interactive visualization of large multi-channel volume datasets where RAM is limited and the OS memory manager can effectively handle page faults [S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Memory Complexity:** Efficient use of physical memory by only loading parts of the data structure actually rendered during tree traversals [S2, unofficial]. However, enforcing whole subtree residency leads to a higher memory footprint compared to partial subtree methods [S1, unofficial].
+- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
+- **Scaling:** Progressive loading advantage: fast recursive refinements due to breadth-first storage [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Efficient use of physical memory, fast recursive refinements, support for multi-channel data.
+- **Limitations:** Higher memory footprint for multiple channels compared to page-table methods, limited to 4 channels due to GPU texture formats, cannot handle occlusions between channels with image-level intermixing.
+- **When to use:** When rendering multi-channel volume datasets that exceed available texture memory and require interactive visualization.
+- **When not to use:** When occlusions between channels need to be handled correctly with image-level intermixing, or when more than 4 channels are required.
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Crassin et al. [CNLE09] as a scheme for out-of-core volume rendering. It has been implemented in the Voreen framework [DLJL22] and extended by Brix et al. [BPH14] to support multi-channel data [S1, unofficial]. The approach leverages OS-level memory mapping (`mmap`) to virtualize memory access to large binary data files [S2, unofficial].
+Originated from Crassin et al. [CNLE09] and extended by Brix et al. [BPH14] for multi-channel data. Implemented in Voreen [DLJL22] [S1, unofficial].
 
